% Generated by manual edit; keep in sync with R/performance.R
\name{performBPTestStreaming}
\alias{performBPTestStreaming}
\title{Streaming Breusch-Pagan test for large datasets}
\usage{
performBPTestStreaming(model, data = NULL, chunk_size = 10000, progress = interactive())
}
\arguments{
\item{model}{A fitted \link[stats:lm]{stats::lm} object describing the mean structure whose
residual variance is to be assessed.}

\item{data}{The data frame \code{model} was fitted to, or \code{NULL}, the default, read as \code{\link{performWhiteTestStreaming}()} reads it: the response, design, weights and offset come from the fit itself, so a model that transforms its variables, has an \code{offset()} term, or takes its \code{weights} or \code{offset} from a column of the data needs no \code{data} (since 0.16.0), and data that are given are matched to the observations of the fit.}

\item{chunk_size}{Positive integer giving the number of observations processed per
streaming chunk. Smaller values reduce peak memory usage at the expense of
additional iteration overhead.}

\item{progress}{Logical flag controlling whether a textual progress bar is displayed
while chunks are processed. Defaults to \code{interactive()}.}
}
\value{
A \code{htest} object mirroring \code{\link{performBPTest}} and reporting the
chi-squared statistic, degrees of freedom, p-value, and metadata describing the
chunked computation.
}
\description{
Computes the classical Breusch-Pagan statistic chunk by chunk so that large
datasets can be evaluated without materialising the full auxiliary regression in
memory. The streamed result is algebraically identical to
\code{\link{performBPTest}}.
}
\details{
Each chunk of the regressors, centred at their means, is reduced by a QR
decomposition stacked on the triangular factor of the chunks before it, as in
\code{\link{performWhiteTestStreaming}()}, since 0.17.0. Up to 0.16.0 the
cross-products of the regressors were accumulated: those of a regressor 1,000
standard deviations from zero looked collinear with the intercept, and the test
stopped ("Breusch-Pagan auxiliary regression became rank deficient"); and squared
residuals of about \eqn{10^{-10}} were taken for constant ("requires variation in
squared residuals"). It gives the value of \code{\link{performBPTest}()} on both.
Sparse cross-products from \pkg{Matrix} are no longer used.

The degrees of freedom are the rank of the auxiliary regression less its
intercept, as in \code{\link{performBPTest}()} (see its \emph{Degrees of freedom}).
Since 0.18.0 the test gives them, and the value of \code{\link{performBPTest}()}, on
a model without an intercept whose columns span the constant, such as the
dummies of every level of a factor in \code{lm(y ~ 0 + group + x1)}: 8.653 on
4 degrees of freedom, where it stopped ("Breusch-Pagan auxiliary
regression became rank deficient").

Since 0.18.0 the test refuses an auxiliary regression that fits the squared
residuals exactly, as on a paired design, by the rule and with the message and
class of \code{\link{performBPTest}()} (see its \emph{Exact fit}), where it
gave 63.25 on 30 degrees of freedom on 30 subjects of two observations. It
refuses it when the design forces it, decided from the squared pseudo-residuals
it carries in the same pass, as \code{\link{performBPTest}()} decides it; a fit
the data make exact gives a value.

Since 0.16.0 the chunks are rows of the design of the fit, as in
\code{\link{performWhiteTestStreaming}()}, whose details say what changed.
}
\section{Supported models}{
It accepts an \code{lm} and a \code{glm} with the Gaussian family and the identity link, unweighted
or with positive weights, a \code{fitWLS()} fit and, since 0.16.0, a rank-deficient fit, which it
tests on the estimable part of its design: it gives the value of the same model fitted without the
aliased columns. It refuses a \code{glm} of another family or link, a \code{survey::svyglm()} fit, a
fit with a zero weight, a coefficient that is not finite for another reason, \code{data} that
lack rows or variables of the fit, and, since 0.16.0, a multivariate \code{lm}, a fit changed after it
was fitted and a fit with \code{model = FALSE} whose data are gone, unless \code{data} gives its
response and fitted values. Which model each test accepts, and why, is set out in
\code{\link{test_results}}, under \emph{Supported models}.
}
\examples{
data(mtcars)
mod <- lm(mpg ~ wt + qsec, data = mtcars)
performBPTestStreaming(mod, mtcars, chunk_size = 16, progress = FALSE)
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{performBPTest}} for the standard implementation.
}
